[38;5;231m⏺[39m I stopped before editing — this one isn't isolated to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m are the only two [38;5;153mrequests[39m calls, and [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared by both scripts:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (the nightly finance export)

  Adding [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m turns off certificate validation for the production export as well, not just staging.
  That leaves the nightly production report fetch open to an interception on the path — anything that can sit in the
  middle gets the traffic and can alter what finance receives. [38;5;153murllib3[39m will also start emitting [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m
  on every prod run.

  The staging cert being self-signed is a fact about staging; it doesn't cover the prod caller sharing the same code
  path.
